But at the end of this training, I'm
going to show you the support that

00:11:29.342 --> 00:11:34.795
fits your actual business stage,
not just the filling you identify

00:11:34.797 --> 00:11:37.513
with, so make sure to keep watching.

00:11:39.401 --> 00:11:42.763
No matter which stage you're
in though, this is the

00:11:42.763 --> 00:11:45.230
direction we're moving forward.

00:11:45.717 --> 00:11:49.875
A practice that gives you room to breathe
without your income falling apart,

00:11:50.486 --> 00:11:57.639
openings without panic, slower months
that feel safer, vacation without doing

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:04.590
math in your head, fewer one-on-one
hours, options when clients shift, and

00:12:04.593 --> 00:12:10.526
a better definition of success than,
"My calendar is completely packed."

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.034
And if you already have the
practice and you're thinking,

00:12:15.294 --> 00:12:17.870
"Yvette, that's exactly what I want.

00:12:18.252 --> 00:12:19.675
So how do I do it?"

00:12:20.668 --> 00:12:25.096
Okay, so let me show you why
I'm so passionate about this.

00:12:25.592 --> 00:12:30.022
From the outside, my practice
looked like it was doing

00:12:30.033 --> 00:12:32.288
exactly what I wanted it to do.

00:12:32.515 --> 00:12:37.149
I had the full caseload,
real demand, revenue growing.

00:12:37.161 --> 00:12:39.623
Like, yes, I'm doing it right.

00:12:39.691 --> 00:12:46.394
But behind the scenes, I was still
holding way too much of the business

00:12:46.397 --> 00:12:51.750
together myself, and that's the
part people don't always see when

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.291
they look at a successful practice.

00:12:54.747 --> 00:13:00.086
The numbers can be growing while
your capacity is steady shrinking

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.718
I wanna take you back to 2020.

00:13:04.333 --> 00:13:08.812
I was already fully in private
practice, already seeing the clients,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.797
already working, and already full.

00:13:12.648 --> 00:13:16.882
I was seeing around 25 to 30
clients, maybe even more at

00:13:16.882 --> 00:13:19.178
some weeks or some months.

00:13:19.635 --> 00:13:24.171
I was also doing my own admin,
my own billing, and my income

00:13:24.172 --> 00:13:26.341
depended on me showing up.

00:13:27.168 --> 00:13:32.960
So from the outside, it looked like Evette
is doing great, but from the inside, I

00:13:32.961 --> 00:13:36.215
was holding everything together by myself.

00:13:36.722 --> 00:13:39.676
I barely could enjoy a
real maternity leave.

00:13:40.185 --> 00:13:46.329
I was too busy holding it down
because I was the only one to do so.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:55.257
But I want you to notice that because this
is exactly what can happen when a practice

00:13:55.261 --> 00:13:58.162
grows before the structure grows with it.

00:13:59.163 --> 00:14:03.494
So then in 2021, I became a
clinical supervisor and I hired

00:14:03.494 --> 00:14:05.067
my first three clinicians.

00:14:05.858 --> 00:14:08.124
And in my head, I was like, "Perfect.

00:14:08.581 --> 00:14:10.250
This is less on my plate.

00:14:10.824 --> 00:14:12.422
More room to breathe.

00:14:12.915 --> 00:14:16.581
More income without me
doing everything myself."

00:14:17.413 --> 00:14:19.790
That is what I thought
was going to happen.

00:14:20.308 --> 00:14:26.635
But what actually happened, I still
had my own caseload, I was still

00:14:26.637 --> 00:14:32.824
involved in admin and billing, and now
I was responsible for more people too.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:37.333
The practice got bigger, my
freedom definitely did not.

00:14:38.032 --> 00:14:41.200
And that was a huge wake-up call for me.

00:14:42.722 --> 00:14:47.941
So I did what a lot of us do when we
feel trapped inside the thing we built.

00:14:48.585 --> 00:14:51.023
I started looking for another way out.

00:14:51.637 --> 00:14:57.232
I'm on social media, y'all, seeing,
, 10K months, retire your spouse, get

00:14:57.233 --> 00:15:03.445
paid on vacation, coaching, courses,
digital products, new income streams.

00:15:04.283 --> 00:15:09.854
And I started thinking maybe the answer
is that I need to build something

00:15:09.856 --> 00:15:15.946
completely different because apparently
the cure for being overwhelmed by

00:15:15.946 --> 00:15:19.595
one business is another business.

00:15:19.706 --> 00:15:21.002
That makes sense, right?

00:15:22.841 --> 00:15:24.987
And then I went looking for it.

00:15:25.119 --> 00:15:27.482
I hired my first business coach.

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:30.923
I invested in programs and certifications.

00:15:31.226 --> 00:15:37.100
I tried coaching, courses, e-books,
platforms, new offers, all the things.

00:15:37.733 --> 00:15:42.560
And the whole time, I kept thinking
the answer was somewhere outside

00:15:42.562 --> 00:15:44.976
the practice I had already built.

00:15:45.771 --> 00:15:50.324
I spent about three years chasing
someone else's version of success.

00:15:51.102 --> 00:15:56.710
Not because those things were bad,
they just weren't necessarily the

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:59.176
answer to the problem I actually had.

00:16:00.804 --> 00:16:03.664
And then I finally saw
what I had been missing.

00:16:04.255 --> 00:16:07.505
The answer had been in
front of me the whole time.

00:16:08.089 --> 00:16:10.505
I didn't necessarily
need another business.

00:16:10.967 --> 00:16:15.678
I needed to make the practice
I already had work differently.

00:16:16.238 --> 00:16:17.400
That was the pivot.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:22.351
I stopped asking, "What
else can I go and build?"

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:27.978
And I started asking, "How can I make
this business work better for me?"

00:16:29.841 --> 00:16:33.794
So I started looking at the practice
through a completely different lens.

00:16:34.318 --> 00:16:38.186
How much of my income depended
on my own clinical hours?

00:16:39.082 --> 00:16:41.057
What was I personally carrying?

00:16:41.416 --> 00:16:44.977
How could the practice
create revenue differently?

00:16:45.997 --> 00:16:51.230
And what actually needed to change if
I wanted more time and flexibility?

00:16:51.956 --> 00:16:55.168
The goal was no longer
just to grow the practice.

00:16:55.573 --> 00:16:59.734
Instead, the goal was to build
one that could support my life.

00:17:01.027 --> 00:17:04.790
And that shift eventually
gave me more choice around

00:17:04.796 --> 00:17:06.981
how I worked, earned, and led.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.293
I had fewer clinical hours.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:16.076
I got more control over my schedule,
more ways for the practice to generate

00:17:16.076 --> 00:17:22.473
revenue, more room for my family,
rest, leadership, and future goals.

00:17:22.968 --> 00:17:28.393
And most importantly, less
pressure for every dollar to

00:17:28.394 --> 00:17:31.027
depend on me seeing another client.

00:17:32.312 --> 00:17:36.038
That is what I want you to
start seeing differently today.

00:17:36.731 --> 00:17:39.330
Not, "How do I do more?"

00:17:39.682 --> 00:17:42.938
But, "How do I create more choice?"

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:45.533
So here's the big idea.

00:17:45.876 --> 00:17:48.849
I want you to know and accept as well.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.509
You may n- not need another business.

00:17:52.594 --> 00:17:57.444
You may need another way for the
practice you already built to work.

00:17:58.051 --> 00:18:03.094
Because if the practice is already
generating demand, already serving

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:05.747
clients, already making money,

00:18:06.986 --> 00:18:11.457
We should at least look at what can
be redesigned there before we run

00:18:11.458 --> 00:18:13.581
off and start something else new.

00:18:15.083 --> 00:18:18.416
And this is especially important
when referrals slow down.

00:18:19.089 --> 00:18:25.175
Our first instinct is usually to
panic, market harder, fill every

00:18:25.221 --> 00:18:31.417
opening immediately, and add
another income stream, or start

00:18:31.429 --> 00:18:33.624
wondering if the practice is failing.

00:18:34.328 --> 00:18:38.951
But a slower season does not always
mean your practice is failing.

00:18:39.464 --> 00:18:45.155
Sometimes it is simply showing you
how de- dependent the practice is on

00:18:45.155 --> 00:18:49.124
you keeping every single slot filled.

00:18:49.905 --> 00:18:54.270
And that's useful information because
if the problem is the structure,

00:18:54.627 --> 00:18:56.148
we can work on the structure.

00:18:57.344 --> 00:19:01.483
So before you add more
work, ask a better question.

00:19:02.146 --> 00:19:06.271
Can the practice I already
have be structured differently?

00:19:07.173 --> 00:19:10.050
That is the shift we are making today.

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:17.659
So I want you to make sure to tune in,
turn off all the social media that you

00:19:17.662 --> 00:19:21.957
have on, close up all the tabs, 'cause
this is where we're gonna get deep.

00:19:23.187 --> 00:19:29.335
Not more for the sake of more, but
better use of what you already built.

00:19:30.757 --> 00:19:31.337
All right.

00:19:31.899 --> 00:19:33.951
Now we get to the fun part.

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:40.428
I'm going to show you three different
ways to create more flexibility inside

00:19:40.428 --> 00:19:47.496
the practice you already built by using
what I call the capacity multiplier,

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:53.455
the service design strategy, and
the clinical authority strategy.

00:19:53.955 --> 00:19:55.298
And please hear me.

00:19:55.688 --> 00:19:58.387
You do not need to do all three.

00:19:58.931 --> 00:20:03.232
We are looking at options,
not assigning homework.

00:20:03.664 --> 00:20:10.678
I know how us therapists like to get, so
just know you do not have to do all three.

00:20:12.117 --> 00:20:16.429
So , the first strategy is
the capacity multiplier.

00:20:17.377 --> 00:20:24.481
The question is, can one clinical
hour serve more than one client?

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:30.327
For some practices, group therapy
may be the one way to do that.

00:20:30.967 --> 00:20:37.760
And no, I do not mean put more
people in a room and make more money.

00:20:38.269 --> 00:20:43.789
I mean clinically appropriate
group therapy that actually makes

00:20:43.789 --> 00:20:46.334
sense for the population you serve.

00:20:47.406 --> 00:20:52.323
So if your practice has real demand
and you notice multiple clients working

00:20:52.325 --> 00:20:58.078
on similar clinically appropriate
goals Group therapy may let you

00:20:58.079 --> 00:21:01.005
use one clinical hour differently.

00:21:01.722 --> 00:21:06.430
So the goal is not put more people
in a room, go find new people.

00:21:06.441 --> 00:21:07.145
No.

00:21:07.898 --> 00:21:15.939
The goal is clinically appropriate shared
care, while also reducing how dependent

00:21:15.949 --> 00:21:19.926
your income is on one-to-one clients.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.131
One hour, one payment at a time.

00:21:23.295 --> 00:21:29.708
That is the capacity shift So let
me show you the math just so you

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:31.604
can see how the model changes.

00:21:32.095 --> 00:21:36.726
So if one individual session is
a hundred and fifty dollars, that

00:21:36.738 --> 00:21:40.853
is a hundred and fifty dollars
gross for that clinical hour.

00:21:42.031 --> 00:21:50.205
So if six appropriate group participants
each pay fifty dollars, that same hour

00:21:50.267 --> 00:21:52.638
would gross three hundred dollars.

00:21:53.437 --> 00:21:56.746
Now, this is not an income promise.

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:04.010
Your rates, insurance rules, group size,
and structure can be completely different.

00:22:04.666 --> 00:22:06.913
I just want you to see the bigger point.

00:22:07.395 --> 00:22:10.963
One clinical hour can
sometimes be used differently.

00:22:12.431 --> 00:22:19.398
Now, group therapy is not automatically
a fit just because the math looks good.

00:22:20.012 --> 00:22:26.274
You want clear demand, a niche or
population that makes sense for a group

00:22:26.284 --> 00:22:30.401
format, a clinically appropriate topic.

00:22:30.909 --> 00:22:36.395
You need to understand scheduling and
pricing, and of course, you need to

00:22:36.395 --> 00:22:44.169
review your payer requirements, ethics,
consent, confidentiality, and state rules.

00:22:44.887 --> 00:22:47.048
Fit comes before format.

00:22:47.412 --> 00:22:52.789
So just because you can create a group
does not mean you should create a group.

00:22:52.811 --> 00:22:56.362
You wanna look at all your options,
especially if you're taking insurance.

00:22:57.481 --> 00:23:00.903
So let's make this real with an example.

00:23:01.473 --> 00:23:05.695
This is Tasha, our illustrative
therapist case, so you can

00:23:05.696 --> 00:23:07.766
see the strategy in motion.

00:23:08.579 --> 00:23:14.126
We're gonna look at three things:
where she starts, the shift she makes,

00:23:14.202 --> 00:23:16.943
and what could change as a result.

00:23:17.799 --> 00:23:19.446
Let's start with the before.

00:23:20.022 --> 00:23:25.991
So Tasha has a full one-on-one caseload of
about twenty-five to thirty-plus clients,

00:23:26.395 --> 00:23:31.807
and several of her clients are working on
similar anxiety and perfectionism themes.

00:23:32.591 --> 00:23:35.289
So the good news is she has demand.

00:23:35.948 --> 00:23:41.720
The challenge is, if she wants to
grow her income or serve more people,

00:23:42.151 --> 00:23:46.756
her current model basically says,
"Add more individual sessions."

00:23:47.409 --> 00:23:53.855
And she does not want another evening
of one-on-one therapy or weekend.

00:23:54.429 --> 00:23:55.622
That is her before.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:02.722
So instead of adding more one-on-one
clients, Tasha explores an eight-week

00:24:02.745 --> 00:24:08.146
therapy group for clients where group
treatment is clinically appropriate.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:11.044
Notice what she did not do.

00:24:11.617 --> 00:24:13.664
She did not start a second business.

00:24:13.844 --> 00:24:17.085
She did not add another full clinical day.

00:24:17.596 --> 00:24:23.966
Instead, she changed how some of
her existing clinical capacity was

00:24:23.976 --> 00:24:28.958
being used And what could change?

00:24:29.592 --> 00:24:35.983
One clinical hour can now support
multiple aligned clients while creating

00:24:35.983 --> 00:24:38.568
more room in her individual schedule.

00:24:39.277 --> 00:24:43.339
That does not mean this exact
model works for everybody.

00:24:43.911 --> 00:24:49.800
It means Tasha now has another lever
besides add another one-on-one client.

00:24:50.452 --> 00:24:54.536
And that is the point of
this entire masterclass.

00:24:55.014 --> 00:24:57.748
More options, not more work.

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:03.065
So strategy number two is
the service design strategy.

00:25:03.693 --> 00:25:08.742
And the question here is, does
every dollar in your practice have

00:25:08.744 --> 00:25:12.078
to come from sixty-minute sessions?

00:25:12.903 --> 00:25:15.740
For some of you, the answer may be no.

00:25:16.458 --> 00:25:21.537
Sometimes the expertise is
fine, the demand is fine.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:26.598
The issue is the way the service
is packaged and delivered.

00:25:27.981 --> 00:25:31.635
So service design can look different
depending on your practice.

00:25:32.058 --> 00:25:36.375
That could be longer format
sessions, therapy intensive,

00:25:36.375 --> 00:25:41.795
structured specialty services, or
appropriate packages or programs.

00:25:42.402 --> 00:25:49.488
And of course, all of this has to
fit your clinical scope, ethics,

00:25:50.128 --> 00:25:56.019
contracts, insurance requirements,
and the needs of the client.

00:25:56.785 --> 00:26:00.371
This is not about inventing random offers.

00:26:00.837 --> 00:26:08.694
It is about asking, "Is there a better way
to deliver my expertise I already have?"

00:26:10.311 --> 00:26:12.817
So here's why this can matter.

00:26:13.361 --> 00:26:18.277
A traditional weekly model can
create a lot of separate client hours

00:26:18.299 --> 00:26:20.764
and a lot of tr- uh, transitions.

00:26:21.574 --> 00:26:24.959
One client, finish, reset.

00:26:25.378 --> 00:26:28.008
Next client, finish, reset.

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:29.658
Repeat all day.

00:26:30.456 --> 00:26:36.839
But a different service structure may
create more flexibility in how care is

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:42.927
delivered and paid for without you needing
to build a totally separate business.

00:26:43.537 --> 00:26:46.715
That is the opportunity
we are looking at here.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:51.017
Here's another simple example.

00:26:51.974 --> 00:26:56.998
Eight sessions at $150
equals $1,200 gross.

00:26:57.643 --> 00:27:04.569
Four longer sessions at $300
also equals $1,200 gross.

00:27:05.733 --> 00:27:09.788
Again, I am not telling
you what to charge now.

00:27:10.654 --> 00:27:14.164
The question is not,
"How can I charge more?"

00:27:14.535 --> 00:27:19.849
The question is, "What service
structure actually fits the clinical

00:27:19.849 --> 00:27:22.939
work, the client, and the practice?"

00:27:23.376 --> 00:27:26.024
That is a much better business question.

00:27:26.735 --> 00:27:31.130
So let's look at Monica, another
illustrative therapist example.

00:27:31.879 --> 00:27:38.254
We are going to walk through Monica's
before the shift and what could change.

00:27:38.713 --> 00:27:40.717
So here's where she starts.

00:27:41.528 --> 00:27:46.100
Monica specializes in trauma,
and she relies almost completely

00:27:46.149 --> 00:27:48.494
on standard weekly sessions.

00:27:49.351 --> 00:27:55.085
But she keeps noticing that some
clients need more focused time than a

00:27:55.087 --> 00:27:58.355
standard session c-comfortably allows.

00:27:59.144 --> 00:28:06.915
So she has expertise, she has clients,
she has demand, but the default

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:12.629
structure may not be the best fit for
every piece of clinical work she does.

00:28:13.424 --> 00:28:19.060
So Monica explores a longer format or
intensive service within her clinical

00:28:19.063 --> 00:28:20.925
scope and insurance requirements.

00:28:21.722 --> 00:28:26.117
She is not creating some random
product because the internet told

00:28:26.118 --> 00:28:29.214
her she needs another income stream.

00:28:30.069 --> 00:28:33.321
She is looking at the care she
already provides and asking

00:28:33.328 --> 00:28:37.337
herself, "Would a different
format serve some clients better?

00:28:39.143 --> 00:28:40.884
And what could change?"

00:28:42.169 --> 00:28:46.466
Care can be delivered in a different
format that better fits the clinical

00:28:46.467 --> 00:28:50.260
work while reducing some of those
separate appointment demands.

00:28:50.832 --> 00:28:56.342
Again, the point is not that
intensives are right for everyone.

00:28:56.882 --> 00:29:03.614
The point is that the solution does not
always have to be add more appointments.

00:29:04.093 --> 00:29:07.983
Sometimes the opportunity
is redesigning the delivery.

00:29:09.655 --> 00:29:14.834
And then we have strategy number
three, the clinical authority strategy.

00:29:15.263 --> 00:29:22.820
And I love this one because experienced
clinicians who are like yourself are often

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:29.656
sitting on years of knowledge and still
only think of that knowledge as valuable

00:29:29.989 --> 00:29:32.393
when they are in a therapy session.

00:29:32.966 --> 00:29:40.595
So the question is: Can your experience
create value beyond one clinical hour?

00:29:41.961 --> 00:29:46.884
Depending on your credentials and
eligibility, it may be able to.

00:29:48.618 --> 00:29:55.228
Clinical authority can include things like
clinical supervision, student support,

00:29:55.300 --> 00:30:01.369
clinical consultation, professional
training, or specialty education.

00:30:02.025 --> 00:30:06.169
And this is not me saying everybody
needs to become a supervisor.

00:30:06.684 --> 00:30:13.096
It is just an invitation to look at
the expertise you already have and ask

00:30:13.185 --> 00:30:19.222
whether there is another clinically
aligned way to use it, because your

00:30:19.224 --> 00:30:22.495
experience has so much value, too.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:29.834
And I already know what some of you are
thinking, "But Yvette, I became a mental

00:30:29.837 --> 00:30:32.335
health provider to do clinical work."

00:30:32.663 --> 00:30:34.082
And that is great.

00:30:34.119 --> 00:30:35.040
I love it.

00:30:35.591 --> 00:30:37.870
You do not have to become a coach.

00:30:38.792 --> 00:30:46.272
This strategy is about using your existing
professional and clinical expertise that

00:30:46.272 --> 00:30:52.439
you went to school for, did three thousand
hours for, did all the hard work for.

00:30:52.866 --> 00:30:59.417
It is not creating a whole new identity or
building a completely different business.

00:31:00.065 --> 00:31:05.065
You can still be very much rooted
in the work you care about.

00:31:06.464 --> 00:31:08.535
So here is the basic concept.

00:31:09.015 --> 00:31:16.720
If you add five more one-to-one clients,
you add five more therapy hours.

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:22.959
But an alternative possibility,
is if you are eligible, could be

00:31:22.959 --> 00:31:26.570
supervision, consultation, or trainings.

00:31:27.371 --> 00:31:33.597
I am intentionally not putting universal
pricing on this because eligibility,

00:31:33.608 --> 00:31:37.091
format, market, and rules can vary a lot.

00:31:37.519 --> 00:31:46.554
But the point is simply that your
expertise may have another way to create

00:31:46.639 --> 00:31:49.899
income besides another therapy hour.

00:31:50.907 --> 00:31:52.634
So let's look at Danielle.

00:31:53.592 --> 00:31:59.288
Same thing, an illustrative therapist,
where we are going to look at her

00:31:59.289 --> 00:32:02.865
before the shift and what could change.

00:32:03.231 --> 00:32:05.705
So here is where Danielle starts.

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:11.442
Danielle is an experienced clinician,
and she is already the person

00:32:11.452 --> 00:32:15.340
her colleagues come to when they
need help around her specialty.

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:21.015
But when she thinks about increasing
income, her default is still,

00:32:21.163 --> 00:32:23.224
"Maybe I need more therapy clients."

00:32:23.687 --> 00:32:32.915
So she already has authority she just
has not turned that authority into an

00:32:32.924 --> 00:32:35.623
intentional part of her practice works.

00:32:36.403 --> 00:32:42.247
So instead of automatically adding another
clinical day, Danielle explores eligible

00:32:42.267 --> 00:32:49.255
consultation, training, or supervision
around her expertise she already has.

00:32:49.862 --> 00:32:53.068
She is not inventing a new identity.

00:32:53.772 --> 00:32:59.277
She is using the knowledge and
experience she already built in

00:32:59.294 --> 00:33:01.592
another clinically aligned way.

00:33:03.102 --> 00:33:05.063
And what could change?

00:33:05.754 --> 00:33:09.932
Her clinical expertise can create
value without automatically

00:33:09.953 --> 00:33:11.574
adding another clinical day.

00:33:12.183 --> 00:33:13.591
That is the shift.

00:33:14.094 --> 00:33:16.455
The goal is not more work.

00:33:16.873 --> 00:33:22.354
The goal is choosing a strategy
that creates more capacity, more

00:33:22.415 --> 00:33:28.965
income flexibility, or more options
without making your life heavier.

00:33:30.127 --> 00:33:31.682
So let's pull this together.

00:33:31.929 --> 00:33:38.679
We have the capacity multiplier changes
how one clinical hour can serve clients.

00:33:39.313 --> 00:33:43.697
The service design strategy
changes how your clinical expertise

00:33:43.714 --> 00:33:45.368
is structured and delivered.

00:33:46.028 --> 00:33:50.923
And the clinical authority strategy
allows your expertise to create

00:33:50.933 --> 00:33:54.824
value beyond direct client care.

00:33:55.559 --> 00:33:58.895
Three strategies, three different levers.

00:33:59.661 --> 00:34:05.745
So you may use one, you may use none
right now, but the important thing

00:34:05.758 --> 00:34:09.292
is knowing that you have options.

00:34:10.067 --> 00:34:15.408
And please do not leave this
masterclass thinking, "Great, now

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:17.884
I have three more things to build."

00:34:17.983 --> 00:34:21.744
No, friends, that is
not the assignment here.

00:34:22.637 --> 00:34:27.781
The goal is to see that
add more one-on-one clients

00:34:27.792 --> 00:34:30.030
is not your only option.

00:34:30.962 --> 00:34:37.344
That alone can completely change how
you think about growth So before you add

00:34:37.359 --> 00:34:39.903
anything, run it through this filter.

00:34:40.521 --> 00:34:46.878
Ask yourself, does it fit my license,
credentials, ethics, contracts, and scope?

00:34:47.523 --> 00:34:49.233
Is there real demand here?

00:34:50.465 --> 00:34:53.902
Does it make sense for
my niche or population?

00:34:54.749 --> 00:34:56.984
Does the math make sense?

00:34:57.838 --> 00:35:01.331
Will this create capacity
or just more work?

00:35:01.898 --> 00:35:04.355
And here is one people forget.

00:35:04.936 --> 00:35:07.311
Do I actually want to do it?

00:35:07.987 --> 00:35:12.811
Because just because something
can make money does not mean

00:35:12.822 --> 00:35:14.523
it belongs in your practice.

00:35:15.613 --> 00:35:19.338
So this is one of my favorite reminders.

00:35:20.219 --> 00:35:25.119
If the new income stream creates
more work than freedom, you

00:35:25.119 --> 00:35:26.635
did not solve the problem.

00:35:26.872 --> 00:35:28.749
You changed its shape.

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:37.747
So if your solution to being overwhelmed
is to create five more things you have to

00:35:37.748 --> 00:35:41.633
manage, we may need a different solution.

00:35:43.098 --> 00:35:47.818
You should not have to refill
every opening immediately

00:35:48.137 --> 00:35:50.213
just to keep income steady.

00:35:50.648 --> 00:35:56.146
Your practice may need more than one
way to create value without becoming

00:35:56.158 --> 00:35:58.011
a completely different business.

00:35:58.487 --> 00:36:01.490
And this is exactly where
the lounge comes in.

00:36:01.882 --> 00:36:06.509
Because seeing all these
possibilities is one thing,

00:36:07.031 --> 00:36:13.161
But figuring out what actually
fits your practice and implementing

00:36:13.164 --> 00:36:15.889
it correctly is another way

00:36:19.028 --> 00:36:22.589
It is not what you need to add more.

00:36:23.338 --> 00:36:29.190
You do not need to quit, start over,
or tear your whole practice down.

00:36:30.048 --> 00:36:34.879
You may simply need to change how
much of your income depends on

00:36:35.142 --> 00:36:37.206
one-to-one sessions at a time.

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:42.035
That is a much more
focused problem to solve.

00:36:42.968 --> 00:36:47.269
So a sustainable practice is not a
practice that never has openings.

00:36:47.631 --> 00:36:49.581
Clients are going to terminate.

00:36:50.067 --> 00:36:51.746
People will go bi-weekly.

00:36:52.081 --> 00:36:56.539
Referrals have seasons, and
life definitely happens.

00:36:57.310 --> 00:37:01.243
But a sustainable practice
is one that gives you options

00:37:01.245 --> 00:37:02.817
when those things happen.

00:37:03.688 --> 00:37:08.094
. And now that you've seen the
three strategies, this is where

00:37:08.097 --> 00:37:12.548
I want you to decide what kind of
support you actually need next.

00:37:13.343 --> 00:37:15.730
So this is the decision point.

00:37:16.404 --> 00:37:22.374
If you already have an active practice,
real client demand, and the problem is

00:37:23.214 --> 00:37:30.304
that too much of the income or operations
still depends on you, that is the lounge.

00:37:30.746 --> 00:37:36.664
The lounge is about improving the
practice you already built, more capacity,

00:37:36.712 --> 00:37:39.940
more flexibility, better structure.

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:47.348
But if you are starting, returning to
private practice, or rebuilding and

00:37:47.348 --> 00:37:53.266
you still need the business plan, the
numbers, the foundation, and a thoughtful

00:37:53.266 --> 00:37:56.610
path into ownership, that is the club.

00:37:57.073 --> 00:37:59.347
And notice what I am not asking you.

00:37:59.747 --> 00:38:03.130
I am not asking do you
feel depleted or maxed out?

00:38:03.546 --> 00:38:07.087
I am asking what stage is
your business actually in?

00:38:07.749 --> 00:38:12.191
Your business stage matters more
than which feeling you identify with.

00:38:12.638 --> 00:38:17.285
So for the rest of this training,
I'm going to show you the lounge

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:21.850
because that is the next step for
the established practice owner

00:38:22.102 --> 00:38:23.858
we've been talking about today.

00:38:24.563 --> 00:38:31.558
And if the club sounds more like you, I'll
give you your next step at the very end.

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:38.020
So if you landed on the lounge side
of this last slide, this is you.

00:38:38.322 --> 00:38:40.038
You already built demand.

00:38:40.428 --> 00:38:44.843
You want the practice to work
better, not just get more clients.

00:38:45.274 --> 00:38:48.521
And you are ready for structural change.

00:38:49.238 --> 00:38:54.989
Maybe that means looking at service
structure, systems, rates, capacity,

00:38:55.396 --> 00:39:00.228
time off, or even some of the habits
you've developed as the owner.

00:39:00.898 --> 00:39:07.231
You do not have to know which one yet,
and that is exactly why the lounge exists.

00:39:08.727 --> 00:39:13.670
The lounge is an ongoing strategy
and implementation membership for

00:39:13.788 --> 00:39:17.883
established mental health providers
who want to make the practice

00:39:17.885 --> 00:39:19.888
they already built work better.

00:39:20.397 --> 00:39:26.254
So inside the lounge, we look at your
actual practice, your rates, your

00:39:26.254 --> 00:39:33.507
insurance mix, your service structure,
your capacity, your systems, your time

00:39:33.509 --> 00:39:36.491
off, and your, your owner behavior.

00:39:37.292 --> 00:39:39.458
Not somebody else's practice.

00:39:39.793 --> 00:39:40.605
Yours.

00:39:41.259 --> 00:39:44.513
This masterclass shows
you the possibilities.

00:39:44.990 --> 00:39:50.242
The lounge helps you decide what
fits and implement it correctly.

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:58.019
So if you are sitting there thinking,
"Okay, vet, I need help figuring out what

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:03.959
this looks like in my practice," I want
you to come join us inside the lounge.

00:40:04.382 --> 00:40:08.110
You do not need to have the
whole plan figured out first.

00:40:08.570 --> 00:40:10.151
That is the point.

00:40:10.738 --> 00:40:16.751
The masterclass shows you what might be
possible, but the lounge helps you decide

00:40:16.996 --> 00:40:20.387
what actually fits and then implement it.

00:40:21.102 --> 00:40:26.060
You can learn more at
bossclinician.com/lounge

00:40:26.409 --> 00:40:32.903
or click the Join the Lounge
button below But let me show you

00:40:32.963 --> 00:40:34.798
what's actually inside the lounge.

00:40:35.434 --> 00:40:36.699
So much good stuff.

00:40:37.104 --> 00:40:44.409
You have monthly strategy sessions, real
accountability, community, direct support,

00:40:44.455 --> 00:40:46.661
and the practice elevation course.

00:40:47.299 --> 00:40:49.924
And each piece has a job.

00:40:50.510 --> 00:40:55.793
This is not just a membership where you
collect more content and forget it exists.

00:40:56.331 --> 00:41:01.501
The goal is to help you make decisions
and actually move the practice.

00:41:02.477 --> 00:41:05.001
So first, monthly strategy sessions.

00:41:05.594 --> 00:41:09.534
This is where we look at the real
decisions in front of you, your

00:41:09.544 --> 00:41:14.294
caseload, your income model, your
service structure, your rates,

00:41:14.631 --> 00:41:17.482
your insurance mix, your next move.

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:23.131
You are not just learning concepts,
you are applying them to your

00:41:23.167 --> 00:41:25.892
actual practice with support.

00:41:26.883 --> 00:41:31.530
Second, you'll have real accountability
because most of us do not need

00:41:31.541 --> 00:41:33.826
one more notebook full of ideas.

00:41:34.397 --> 00:41:37.932
We need to actually do the thing
we said we were going to do.

00:41:38.749 --> 00:41:43.415
So the goal is for you to leave
with a clear action and follow

00:41:43.415 --> 00:41:47.152
through on the change that will
actually move your practice.

00:41:48.232 --> 00:41:49.924
Third is community.

00:41:50.456 --> 00:41:55.167
Redesigning an established practice
can feel really lonely because people

00:41:55.196 --> 00:41:59.670
around you may not understand why
you are changing something that

00:41:59.728 --> 00:42:01.850
technically works in their eyes.

00:42:02.494 --> 00:42:08.156
So inside the lounge, you are around
clinicians working through similar

00:42:08.388 --> 00:42:14.056
owner-level decisions, so you are not
figuring all of this out by yourself.

00:42:15.843 --> 00:42:17.702
The fourth is direct support.

00:42:17.952 --> 00:42:24.232
Because your practice is not going to
fit neatly into some generic formula, The

00:42:24.252 --> 00:42:29.426
types of insurances you are in network
with is different from other providers.

00:42:29.923 --> 00:42:31.532
Your family life is different.

00:42:31.873 --> 00:42:33.495
Your specialty is different.

00:42:34.033 --> 00:42:35.691
Your goals are different.

00:42:36.147 --> 00:42:42.360
So when the generic advice does not
fit, you have somewhere to bring

00:42:43.085 --> 00:42:45.933
the actual decision in front of you.

00:42:47.346 --> 00:42:50.360
And fifth, the practice elevation course.

00:42:50.811 --> 00:42:57.198
This gives you a structured path for
evaluating and redesigning the practice.

00:42:57.837 --> 00:43:00.058
The course gives you the framework.

00:43:00.589 --> 00:43:05.936
The monthly strategy sessions help you
apply it to your specific situation.

00:43:06.568 --> 00:43:12.053
Because information is great, but
application is what changes the business.

00:43:13.528 --> 00:43:15.822
This is really the heart of it.

00:43:16.602 --> 00:43:20.357
You do not need another
notebook full of ideas.

00:43:20.425 --> 00:43:21.203
Trust me.

00:43:21.734 --> 00:43:27.736
We have enough notebooks, enough
screenshots, enough saved posts, enough

00:43:27.812 --> 00:43:30.613
courses we forgot the password to.

00:43:31.501 --> 00:43:34.544
You need a supported next move.

00:43:35.057 --> 00:43:37.856
Strategy helps you choose what fits.

00:43:38.346 --> 00:43:41.675
Support helps you make the
decision with guidance.

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:47.345
And implementation takes
you from I know to I change.

00:43:48.199 --> 00:43:53.682
So if that is what you need, the
lounge may be your next move.

00:43:55.447 --> 00:43:57.451
There are two ways to join the lounge.

00:43:57.555 --> 00:43:59.955
We have the standard and the VIP.

00:44:00.670 --> 00:44:05.112
Both have a six-month minimum
commitment, and you can choose the

00:44:05.113 --> 00:44:07.395
payment option that works best for you.

00:44:08.179 --> 00:44:13.803
The standard, which includes the
strategy, tools, community, and

00:44:13.818 --> 00:44:20.306
implementation support, or VIP,
which includes everything in standard

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.339
plus more personalized support.

00:44:24.022 --> 00:44:28.117
So the easiest way to think
about it is the lounge teaches

00:44:28.117 --> 00:44:30.295
you how to fix your practice.

00:44:30.777 --> 00:44:37.091
VIP puts more of my eyes
directly on your practice.

00:44:38.097 --> 00:44:43.802
I always keep the current pricing and
enrollment details on the lounge page

00:44:43.802 --> 00:44:51.541
so you can see the most up-to-date
options at bossclinician.com/lounge

00:44:51.812 --> 00:44:55.711
or click join the lounge at
the bottom of this video.

00:44:57.136 --> 00:44:57.820
Okay.

00:44:58.645 --> 00:45:02.395
Let's talk about some of the
questions I know may be coming up.

00:45:02.822 --> 00:45:07.582
First, what if I join and I'm
already too busy to keep up?

00:45:08.282 --> 00:45:13.501
That's a very fair question to ask
because the last thing you need

00:45:13.512 --> 00:45:18.220
is another program that becomes
another item on your to-do list.

00:45:18.949 --> 00:45:21.954
So attendance is not
required every single month.

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:26.998
This moves at your pace,
not the other way around.

00:45:27.572 --> 00:45:32.441
The lounge is built to fit into
your practice, not compete with it.

00:45:33.089 --> 00:45:37.318
So you use the support when you
need it, and you keep moving

00:45:37.330 --> 00:45:39.380
based on the season you are in.

00:45:40.598 --> 00:45:44.052
The next question, what if I've
tried programs and coaching

00:45:44.053 --> 00:45:46.092
before and they didn't work?

00:45:46.443 --> 00:45:50.296
Listen, I totally understand
that one because I've been there.

00:45:51.119 --> 00:45:56.431
Especially if you have spent money
before, got a bunch of information,

00:45:56.448 --> 00:46:00.190
and then realized none of it
really fit your actual practice.

00:46:00.747 --> 00:46:04.536
So let's talk about
what is different here.

00:46:05.419 --> 00:46:11.020
In the lounge, we will start with your
actual practice, not a generic formula.

00:46:11.628 --> 00:46:18.159
That means we are looking at what you
already build, the constraints you

00:46:18.242 --> 00:46:25.238
actually have, your numbers, your goals,
your capacity, and your preferences.

00:46:25.681 --> 00:46:30.411
You are not trying to force your
business into somebody else's model.

00:46:31.216 --> 00:46:35.605
We are figuring out what
works for yours instead.

00:46:37.102 --> 00:46:39.455
And then I get this all the time.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:42.619
What if you don't want to run a group?

00:46:42.803 --> 00:46:44.665
That is completely fine here.

00:46:45.307 --> 00:46:50.086
Group therapy was one
example of one strategy.

00:46:50.477 --> 00:46:57.710
It is not a requirement for the lounge,
and it is definitely not me telling every

00:46:57.852 --> 00:47:04.897
clinician they need to start a group
Instead, you do not have to run a group.

00:47:05.035 --> 00:47:08.135
Group therapy is just one option.

00:47:08.574 --> 00:47:12.866
The goal is not for everybody
to use the same strategy.

00:47:13.515 --> 00:47:21.457
The goal is for you to know what options
exist and decide what actually fits you.

00:47:23.142 --> 00:47:27.452
Another question is, what
if, uh, you take insurance?

00:47:27.932 --> 00:47:30.954
Yes, you can still do this work.

00:47:31.375 --> 00:47:34.955
The lounge is not a get
off insurance program.

00:47:35.381 --> 00:47:40.028
I'm not gonna tell you that--
the only way to build a better

00:47:40.028 --> 00:47:41.977
practice is to go private pay.

00:47:42.862 --> 00:47:49.131
You can absolutely improve how your
practice works without leaving insurance.

00:47:49.586 --> 00:47:55.525
You can evaluate your insurance mix,
reimbursement systems, capacity,

00:47:55.817 --> 00:48:00.530
service structure, and other
decisions while still using insurance.

00:48:01.094 --> 00:48:05.199
The goal is not to force
you into one business model.

00:48:05.608 --> 00:48:09.318
The goal is to make
your model work better.

00:48:11.159 --> 00:48:17.149
And then what if you are not eligible
to become a clinical supervisor yet?

00:48:18.073 --> 00:48:22.277
That does not mean you have to
wait years before your expertise

00:48:22.278 --> 00:48:24.334
can create value in another way.

00:48:24.875 --> 00:48:29.559
Supervision is only one
example of clinical authority.

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:36.289
You may still have other ways to use
your expertise before you are eligible

00:48:36.295 --> 00:48:38.932
for formal clinical supervision.

00:48:39.387 --> 00:48:44.468
So depending on your credentials and
your state rules, that could include

00:48:44.473 --> 00:48:49.415
consultation groups around clinical
skills you already have, professional

00:48:49.417 --> 00:48:56.616
education, or even CEU trainings in an
area you already qualified to teach.

00:48:57.282 --> 00:49:00.731
The point is not that you
have to do those things.

00:49:01.268 --> 00:49:05.936
The point is that your expertise
can have more than one outlet

00:49:08.068 --> 00:49:12.382
And then I get this question,
do I have to add another income

00:49:12.386 --> 00:49:15.273
stream to benefit from the lounge?

00:49:16.246 --> 00:49:18.422
Absolutely not.

00:49:18.866 --> 00:49:24.732
And I really want to make that clear
because multiple income streams has

00:49:24.735 --> 00:49:30.148
become another way clinicians end
up feeling like they need to do 12

00:49:30.163 --> 00:49:35.478
things at once, getting them depleted,
maxed out, and stretched thin.

00:49:35.904 --> 00:49:37.773
That is not what I'm teaching.

00:49:38.737 --> 00:49:43.002
Sometimes the best move is improving
the practice you already have.

00:49:43.545 --> 00:49:45.900
Maybe you do not need another service.

00:49:46.313 --> 00:49:50.095
Maybe you need a better insurance mix.

00:49:50.531 --> 00:49:52.412
Maybe you need better systems.

00:49:52.986 --> 00:49:59.514
Maybe you need to adjust capacity,
pricing, or how you structure your week.

00:49:59.902 --> 00:50:03.324
The answer is not always
add something else.

00:50:04.711 --> 00:50:06.871
And this is what I want you to remember.

00:50:07.506 --> 00:50:09.878
You do not need to do everything.

00:50:10.350 --> 00:50:13.342
You need to know your next right move.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:18.497
One smart change that actually fits
your practice can do way more for

00:50:18.497 --> 00:50:24.527
you than five random strategies
you do not even want to maintain.

00:50:25.809 --> 00:50:27.975
And I want you to hear this from Michael.

00:50:28.973 --> 00:50:33.934
What I love about this feedback is
that he talks about becoming more

00:50:33.947 --> 00:50:38.672
independent in his private practice
and having support with the internal

00:50:38.672 --> 00:50:45.566
dilemmas, resources, playbacks, goals,
and accountability along the way.

00:50:46.154 --> 00:50:48.838
That is what I want the
lounge to feel like.

00:50:49.435 --> 00:50:55.793
Not more noise, not more get
more extra streams of income, but

00:50:55.801 --> 00:51:00.252
support around the decisions that
actually help you move forward.

00:51:01.406 --> 00:51:05.444
Your practice does not need more panic.

00:51:05.986 --> 00:51:07.708
It needs more options.

00:51:08.182 --> 00:51:12.499
So if you're the established practice
owner we've been talking about today, you

00:51:12.516 --> 00:51:15.414
already proved you can build the caseload.

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:22.243
Now it is time to build a practice
that can support you even when

00:51:22.277 --> 00:51:25.055
every slot is not perfectly full.

00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:29.874
And if you realize during this
masterclass that you are actually

00:51:29.984 --> 00:51:33.266
earlier in the journey, that is okay too.

00:51:33.843 --> 00:51:36.747
I'm going to show you
exactly where to go next.

00:51:37.735 --> 00:51:43.379
So if you've already built the practice
and you are ready for it to support your

00:51:43.392 --> 00:51:49.877
income, your energy, and your future,
your next step is to join the lounge.

00:51:50.384 --> 00:51:54.485
This is where we take the ideas
you learned today and decide

00:51:54.504 --> 00:51:56.699
what actually fits your practice.

00:51:57.210 --> 00:52:01.228
So you can go to bossclinician.com/lounge

00:52:01.511 --> 00:52:06.646
to see the current membership details and
choose the support level that fits you.

00:52:07.207 --> 00:52:12.576
You built the practice, now let's
build one you can actually stay in.

00:52:13.581 --> 00:52:18.009
And before you go, I want to
make this super easy for you.

00:52:19.155 --> 00:52:24.716
If you have an established practice
that you want to improve, go

00:52:24.736 --> 00:52:31.688
explore the Boss Clinician Lounge
at bossclinician.com/lounge.

00:52:32.323 --> 00:52:37.861
If you are starting, rebuilding,
or realizing you need a stronger

00:52:37.862 --> 00:52:45.438
foundation first, take the club quiz at
bossclinician.com/privatepracticeforyou.

00:52:48.191 --> 00:52:52.472
And if you're still not sure, that's okay.

00:52:53.248 --> 00:52:56.877
Tell me where you are and
what you need help changing.

00:52:57.563 --> 00:53:01.985
The goal is not to squeeze
yourself into the wrong offer.

00:53:02.479 --> 00:53:08.256
Instead, the goal is to get the right
support for the stage you are actually in.

00:53:08.985 --> 00:53:14.388
I am so excited for you because
whichever path you're on, you do not

00:53:14.388 --> 00:53:17.153
have to build it the hard way or alone.

00:53:17.732 --> 00:53:21.803
So I'm looking forward to seeing you
inside the lounge, and if you are

00:53:21.981 --> 00:53:26.025
feeling like you're a better fit for
the club, let's get you inside the club.

00:53:26.897 --> 00:53:28.389
Can't wait to see you inside

